    "CorrectChoice": "A",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Price elasticity of demand = % change Q / % change P. Rearranging: % change Q = Elasticity x % change P = -2.0 x 15% = -30%. Quantity demanded decreases by 30%. Revenue impact: new revenue as a fraction of old revenue = (1 + % change P) x (1 + % change Q) = (1.15) x (0.70) = 0.805. Revenue decreases by 19.5% (from 100% to 80.5% of original). This confirms the demand is elastic (|E| > 1), meaning a price increase reduces total revenue. Bridgewater should not raise the price if revenue maximization is the objective. A common error is multiplying the elasticity by the price change without considering the compound effect on revenue.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "This answer incorrectly applies the elasticity: it uses -1.0 instead of -2.0, yielding a 15% quantity decrease. With a 15% price increase and 15% quantity decrease, revenue changes by (1.15)(0.85) = 0.9775, a 2.25% decrease - not a 15% increase. The candidate may be confusing elasticity with a 1:1 relationship between price and quantity.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "This answer correctly computes the 30% quantity decrease but incorrectly concludes revenue increases. Revenue = Price x Quantity. If price rises 15% but quantity falls 30%, the quantity effect dominates: (1.15)(0.70) = 0.805, a 19.5% decrease. Revenue increases only when demand is inelastic (|E| < 1).",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "This answer uses an elasticity of -0.5 instead of -2.0, yielding a 7.5% quantity decrease. With elastic demand (|E| > 1), the quantity response is proportionally larger than the price change, not smaller. The candidate may be dividing the elasticity by 2 or misreading the elasticity value.",
