    "ExplanationCorrect": "Bundle pricing analysis requires comparing the contribution with the bundle against the contribution without it. Without the bundle, expected contribution per customer = 0.50 x ($800 - $320) + 0.30 x ($600 - $240) + 0.20 x ($400 - $160) = 0.50 x $480 + 0.30 x $360 + 0.20 x $240 = $240 + $108 + $48 = $396. With the bundle, contribution per customer = $1,200 - ($320 + $240 + $160) = $1,200 - $720 = $480. Incremental contribution = $480 - $396 = $84. The bundle generates $84 more per customer than the individual-purchase scenario. A common error is comparing the bundle contribution ($480) against zero, ignoring that some customers would have purchased individual tools anyway.",
    "ExplanationWrongA": "$480.00 is the bundle contribution per customer ($1,200 - $720 = $480) without subtracting the contribution that would have been earned from individual sales. This answer ignores cannibalization - the fact that 100% of customers would have purchased something individually even without the bundle.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "$396.00 is the expected contribution per customer without the bundle. This answer represents the baseline, not the incremental benefit. The question asks for the incremental contribution from offering the bundle, which requires comparing both scenarios.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "$540.00 results from computing the revenue difference ($1,200 - $660 = $540) without subtracting variable costs. The $660 is the expected individual revenue per customer (0.50 x $800 + 0.30 x $600 + 0.20 x $400 = $660). Revenue differences overstate the economic benefit because they ignore the variable costs that increase when all three tools are delivered.",
    "FormulaReference": "DA-08: Bundle pricing - Incremental contribution = Bundle contribution - Expected individual contribution",
