    "QuestionID": "P2-C-628", "question_state": "Unprocessed", "Part2OnlyFlag": true,
    "ItemStyle": "single-select", "UniqueConceptKey": "C-628-dynamic-pricing-profit-maximization",
    "Stem": "Bridgewater Electronics sells premium noise-cancelling headphones. At a price of $150, monthly demand is 2,000 units. At $120, demand increases to 3,200 units. At $140, demand is 2,400 units. At $130, demand is 2,800 units. Variable cost is $60 per unit, and monthly fixed costs are $80,000. Which price point maximizes monthly operating income?",
    "Choices": {"A": "$130, yielding operating income of $116,000", "B": "$150, yielding operating income of $100,000", "C": "$120, yielding operating income of $112,000", "D": "$140, yielding operating income of $112,000"},
    "CorrectChoice": "A",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Dynamic pricing requires evaluating profit at each price point. At $150: (150 - 60) x 2,000 - 80,000 = 90 x 2,000 - 80,000 = $100,000. At $140: (140 - 60) x 2,400 - 80,000 = 80 x 2,400 - 80,000 = $112,000. At $130: (130 - 60) x 2,800 - 80,000 = 70 x 2,800 - 80,000 = $116,000. At $120: (120 - 60) x 3,200 - 80,000 = 60 x 3,200 - 80,000 = $112,000. The profit-maximizing price is $130, generating $116,000 in operating income. Note that $120 and $140 both yield $112,000 - the same profit from different price-volume combinations. A common error is selecting the highest price ($150) or the highest volume ($120) without computing profit at each point.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "$150 yields the highest per-unit contribution ($90) but the lowest volume (2,000 units). Total contribution = $180,000, minus $80,000 fixed costs = $100,000. This is the lowest profit among the four options. The high price suppresses demand enough to reduce total profit.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "$120 yields the highest volume (3,200 units) but the lowest per-unit contribution ($60). Total contribution = $192,000, minus $80,000 = $112,000. While volume is high, the low margin limits total profit. This price is $4,000 less profitable than $130.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "$140 yields the same profit as $120 ($112,000) but at a higher price and lower volume. The contribution margin per unit is $80, and volume is 2,400 units. This demonstrates that different price-volume combinations can yield identical profits - the profit curve is symmetric around the optimum.",
    "FormulaReference": "DA-01: Profit = (Price - Variable cost) x Quantity - Fixed costs",
    "LOSTag": "C.7", "BlueprintDomain": "Decision Analysis",
    "Difficulty": "Moderate", "DifficultyScore": 3, "CognitiveLevel": "Apply", "CalculationItem": true,
    "CommonTrapReference": "Selecting the highest price or highest volume without computing profit at each point",
    "Authorities": ["Dynamic pricing theory", "IMA SMA on relevant costing"],
    "source_ids": ["DA-01: Profit maximization under price-volume tradeoff"],
    "source_support_for_key": {"source_id": "DA-01: Profit maximization under price-volume tradeoff", "rule_or_proposition": "Profit = (P - VC) x Q - FC. Evaluate profit at each candidate price to identify the maximum.", "application_to_facts": "At $130: (130-60)x2,800-80,000 = $116,000 - the highest among all price points.", "key_conclusion": "The profit-maximizing price is $130, yielding $116,000 in operating income."},
    "distractor_intent": {"B": {"misconception": "Highest price yields highest profit", "why_plausible": "The $90 per-unit contribution is the highest, but demand is suppressed to 2,000 units", "tier_candidate": 1}, "C": {"misconception": "Highest volume yields highest profit", "why_plausible": "3,200 units is the highest demand, but the $60 margin limits total contribution", "tier_candidate": 1}, "D": {"misconception": "Middle price is always optimal", "why_plausible": "$140 yields $112,000 - the same as $120 - demonstrating the profit curve symmetry", "tier_candidate": 2}},
    "uniqueness_note": "The $130 price uniquely maximizes profit at $116,000, exceeding both the $120 and $140 options by $4,000.",
    "source_status": "RESOLVED", "hold_reason": "", "schema_version": "1.1",
    "VerifiedChecks": ["Part2OnlyFlag verified true", "EW[CC] empty", "Non-CC EW slots >= 75 chars", "No boilerplate text", "Difficulty justified", "Independent answer derived: $130 -> $116,000 (verified all four scenarios)", "Authority citations match tested concept"]
